<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">This is coming from our District Ranger Michele Holman if we every run into this, (it has not been an issue here but good info).</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">People have the right to film public employees performing job duties on public property.&nbsp; If you see someone filming, I think the best thing to do is leave them alone.&nbsp; You could say hello and
 ask them if they need any help or directions. If they say no- then leave them alone.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">I would be surprised if we see any of these folks...but if we do, they will likely be outside on the sidewalks or parking lots and maybe even in the lobbies.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">We don't want to end up on a YouTube channel about violating first amendment rights.&nbsp; We do want to call our LEOs to notify them that there is a first amendment auditor.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p class="x_MsoNormal"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; color:black">&nbsp;</span></p>
</div>
